<DocsLayout title="Audit log" activeNav="/platform/audit-log">
  <h1>Audit log</h1>
  <p>
    The audit log records changes to the workspace: what changed, who changed it, from where, and
    what the value was before. It is written automatically — nothing opts in, and nothing can opt
    out.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="audit-log"
    alt="Audit log with filters for applications, actors, events, sources and a date range, listing recent changes with before and after values"
    caption="Each entry carries the actor, the source, and the change itself."
  />

  <h2>What an entry tells you</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Column</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Activity</td>
        <td>
          The record and what happened to it, with the change inline —
          <code>Failures: 2 → 3</code>, or a full before-and-after for list values such as webhook
          subscriptions.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Actor</td>
        <td>The person or API key responsible, or <strong>System</strong> for platform-initiated changes such as delivery retries.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Source</td>
        <td><code>web</code> for the console, <code>api</code> for an API key, <code>System</code> for background work — with the IP address.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>When</td>
        <td>Timestamp of the change.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The distinction between <code>web</code> and <code>api</code> is the one people reach for most:
    it separates a person clicking in the console from a pipeline acting with a key, and the actor
    column then names which key.
  </p>

  <h2>Filters</h2>
  <p>
    Five filters combine: application, actor, event type, source, and a date range. They are
    designed around the questions you actually arrive with:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>"Who touched this app last week?"</strong> — filter by application and set the date
      range. Application filtering follows the app's identifiers too, so an identifier change shows
      up under its app rather than only under its own record.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"What has this API key been doing?"</strong> — filter by actor. Useful before revoking
      a key you no longer recognise.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>"Did anything change outside the console?"</strong> — filter Source to
      <code>api</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Scope and retention</h2>
  <p>
    The log is scoped to the current workspace: entries from other teams are never visible here,
    whatever the actor's other memberships.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Only owners and admins can open this screen.</strong> Contributors and viewers get
    <code>403</code> — the sidebar entry is there, but the page is not theirs to read. If you need a
    teammate to investigate a change, their role has to allow it; see
    <a href="/platform/team-access">Team access</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Secret values are excluded by design. A credential change is recorded as a change, but the
    encrypted payload is not written into the audit trail — otherwise the log would become a second,
    less protected copy of every secret in the vault.
  </p>

  <h2>What is recorded</h2>
  <p>
    Applications, identifiers, credentials, integrations, pipelines, webhook endpoints and their
    subscriptions, notification destinations, team membership and roles, and API key lifecycle. Build
    and artifact activity also appears in an app-scoped form on the
    <a href="/platform/applications">application overview</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reads are not recorded as ordinary entries, with one exception: revealing credential material is
    an access event and is written down. That is deliberate — knowing who read a keystore matters as
    much as knowing who changed it.
  </p>

  <h2>No API yet</h2>
  <p>
    The audit log is console-only; there is no API v1 endpoint and therefore no CLI command. To ship
    changes into an external SIEM today, use
    <a href="/platform/integrations/webhooks">webhooks</a>, which cover the same lifecycle events as
    they happen.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
